<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bernhard III, Duke of Saxe-Meiningen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1851">1851</a>–<a href="/years/1928">1928</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bernhard III, Duke of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a>, was the last reigning duke of Saxe-Meiningen, succeeding his father George II in 1914. A Prussian general and a scholar of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, he was married to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princess-charlotte-of-wales">Princess Charlotte</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a>. He abdicated in November 1918 during the German Revolution, and the duchy became a free state that joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> in 1920.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bernhard-iii-duke-of-saxe-meiningen.jpg" alt="Bernhard III, Duke of Saxe-Meiningen" width="320" height="501" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis Otto Weber · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bernhard_III_(Saxe-Meiningen).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bernhard was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a> on 1 April 1851, the eldest son of Georg II, Duke of Saxe-Meiningen, and his first wife, Princess Charlotte of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. He was taught from 1860 by Prof. Rossmann and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-university">Heidelberg University</a> in 1869, breaking off his studies to serve as an orderly officer in the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and resuming them afterwards at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>.</p><p>On 18 February 1878 he married, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, his second cousin Princess Charlotte of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, daughter of Frederick III, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">German Emperor</a>, and granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>. Their only child, Princess Feodora, married Heinrich XXX of Reuss-Köstritz in 1898. Queen Marie of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, his wife's cousin, wrote that Charlotte never liked him, though he was devoted to her.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1873 Bernhard served again in the army and rose to its highest ranks. By 1905 he was Generaloberst and inspector general of the 2nd Army Inspection, covering central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and in 1909 he was given the rank of Generaloberst im Range eines Generalfeldmarschalls before retiring from active service in 1912. Beside soldiering he was a composer, poet and translator, an expert on Modern Greek known for translating German literature into Greek. His historical studies, for which he travelled repeatedly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, earned him an honorary doctorate from the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Breslau</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Bernhard became duke on his father's death in 1914. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> began he hoped to be given command of an army, and when he was not, he withdrew from his part in the duchy's government. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat the German Revolution forced him to abdicate on 10 November 1918, and like all the German princes he lost his title and his state. He spent the rest of his life in his former country as a private citizen, died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a> on 16 January 1928, and was buried beside his wife in the park at Altenstein.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bernhard III of Saxe-Meiningen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Bernhard III</b> abdicated in November 1918, and in 1920 the state merged into the Free State of Thuringia.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-meiningen">Duchy of Saxe-Meiningen</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.55,10.42&amp;year=1901" aria-label="Meiningen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Bernhard III, Duke of Saxe-Meiningen"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5563 5760h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5563 5760h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.55,10.42&amp;year=1901">What was here in 1901? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
